#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133398 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Selecting a Specific Value For Which a Solution Is Guaranteed"}] :question/id #uuid "bb373a5d-aa71-58ed-82e3-b74a8f51d144" :question/math-academy-id "e-114" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-3)^2+2.$ According to the intermediate value theorem, for which values of $k$ does the equation $f(x)=k$ have a guaranteed solution on the interval $[2,6]?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by plotting the function.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/02c989a33b6f027c0814d39d1468379dcb24f90536257b2b0bf4031191a73f85.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,6],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(2)$ and $f(6).$\n\nSince $f(2)=3$ and $f(6)=11$, we must have \n$k\\in[3,11].$\n\n**Watch out!** According to the graph of this particular function, $f(x)=k$ may have solutions for $k < 3,$ but these are *not* guaranteed by the intermediate value theorem. The theorem only guarantees the solutions for $k$ between $f(a)$ and $f(b).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133399 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Number of Solutions Given a Table of Values of a Continuous Function"}] :question/id #uuid "d048aae2-5773-5da7-ab18-35d41bfbb53a" :question/math-academy-id "e-115" :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in[-2,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=-2?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-2,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-2,2].$\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[-2,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-2},{\\color{blue}-1}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}-2})={\\color{red}4}$ and $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}.$ Since $-2 \\not \\in [{\\color{red}-1},{\\color{red}4}],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-1},{\\color{blue}0}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}$ and $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}.$ Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}-1}],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}0},{\\color{blue}1}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}$ and $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}.$ Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}5}],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}1},{\\color{blue}2}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}$ and $g({\\color{blue}2})={\\color{red}3}.$ Since $-2 \\not \\in[{\\color{red}3},{\\color{red}5}],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=-2$ on the subintervals $[-1,0]$ and $[0,1].$ Therefore, $g(x)=-2$ has at least two solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133402 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied"}] :question/id #uuid "89750383-d5a6-5bf4-b6b2-abd489e44d3c" :question/math-academy-id "e-116" :question/problem "On which type of closed intervals can  the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function \n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{x^2+1}{x^2-1} \\, ?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have $x^2-1=0,$ which corresponds to $x=1$ or $x=-1.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain points $x=1$ or $x=-1.$ \n\nFor instance, the intervals $[2,4]$ or $[-0.5,0.5]$ are allowed, but the intervals $[-2,0]$ and $[0.5, 1.5]$ are not."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133570 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d01d143-68f2-534a-8be6-4f63f9ab0d40" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement On the Intermediate Value Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "e32fb82c-2e8f-5383-b15a-8f98aa86b716" :question/math-academy-id "e-6753" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/c79e9567742a64066dfe42651f3a4e05847bd5c8b7d8ee616238010a2926c087.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $x\\in [-5,2].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\textrm{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in [-5,2]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-5,2],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-5)$ and $f(2).$\n\nSince $f(-5)=1$ and $f(2)=4$, we must have $k\\in[1,4].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$1$ and $4$\". The complete sentence is:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $1$ and $4$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in [-5,2]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d01d143-68f2-534a-8be6-4f63f9ab0d40" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement On the Intermediate Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3a17129-1fa0-5604-b551-0c00c0542d13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344965} {:answer/id #uuid "b4903b58-bb4f-5763-9e52-008e9a73ef1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344966} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd245c5-4f9f-5a79-90ca-2231c63d6b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344967} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa21923-5820-59c1-90a2-642ed3181a6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344968} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6d251b-87fe-5549-bfda-ea80b41e7e77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4903b58-bb4f-5763-9e52-008e9a73ef1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344966} :answer-field/id #uuid "681b180b-90ad-5629-a0cf-c735303edefb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10c6d9fe-4c2a-5584-b952-8a2869149fea" :question/math-academy-id "q-105630" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/5e99e02134a5f42aa440cd2d83c971afee3cdb3c210b4488ebccf5cfff1ef8ba.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[-3,0].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [-3,0]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-3,0],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-3)$ and $f(0)$ inclusively.\n\nSince $f(-3)=-1$ and $f(0)=1$, we must have \n$k\\in [-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$-1$ and $1$\".\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/50f937db1a3a87b1887eb22851b285190f1a8999c986af7f1cf32a1676873da2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Selecting a Specific Value For Which a Solution Is Guaranteed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0102cda0-22e1-56e5-8393-87d5b2c14584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344972} {:answer/id #uuid "994ad0fd-03e3-55e5-bfa0-3857c58887e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344973} {:answer/id #uuid "256b7d39-0f73-5b2a-9d56-a09efaae8def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344974} {:answer/id #uuid "fcbd4e98-2c6a-5b1e-a9f9-28008db41912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344975} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7d10be-65d4-5b60-bdcc-bb979c1c73a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "256b7d39-0f73-5b2a-9d56-a09efaae8def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344974} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3abd14a-2835-5213-934b-f4ee97ede681" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c212e7c8-14a4-5162-9693-5870da3002e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-105666" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=2-{x}^{2}.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $x\\in [-3,1]?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-3,1],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-3)$ and $f(1).$\n\nSince $f(-3)=-7$ and $f(1)=1,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-7,1].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=-2$ belongs to the interval $[-7,1].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/3a9cf79e319e3a4a8d7d5195d65bc01e69bceb3721c29ca905132c1a607ba413.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Selecting a Specific Value For Which a Solution Is Guaranteed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f13b08ba-d624-5183-9813-ae2879eb1291" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344979} {:answer/id #uuid "c49851eb-00b0-58fb-8cc7-ad896c139ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344980} {:answer/id #uuid "807fe3df-ebb5-5636-8b13-6f8024967c4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344981} {:answer/id #uuid "746c1e84-e34f-5197-9128-967f77117a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344982} {:answer/id #uuid "f2735f17-a908-5f7d-8d25-e224647147f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2735f17-a908-5f7d-8d25-e224647147f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344983} :answer-field/id #uuid "d475312d-3f30-5553-bd62-b0f81030b07d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "84e2e7d1-ebc5-58ab-8dc8-1f209b929dba" :question/math-academy-id "q-105684" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+\\sqrt{x}.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem **not** guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $[1,4]?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[1,4],$ therefore, the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(1)$ and $f(4).$\n\nSince $f(1)=2$ and $f(4)=66,$ we must have \n$k\\in [2,66].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=1$ does not belong to the interval $[2,66].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Number of Solutions Given a Table of Values of a Continuous Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d044327e-10ef-5f42-a791-2c1c006fb57e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344986} {:answer/id #uuid "fb430401-9119-5b8c-896c-d1e45a5dff02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344987} {:answer/id #uuid "b67ecf48-b76f-5d47-8c4d-011d0b118177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344988} {:answer/id #uuid "36d2032f-b374-5d08-9044-af2a46fc1cf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344989} {:answer/id #uuid "589418da-2615-552b-821c-9ed20807450e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d044327e-10ef-5f42-a791-2c1c006fb57e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344986} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e63cee1-8f88-5bfc-a6ed-546bee2045b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07c30a16-0504-5c16-ba6b-3435983938c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-105701" :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [2,7].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $-3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=-1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[2,7]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[2,7].$\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[2,7]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1.$\n\n- On the subinterval $[2,3],$ we have $g(2)=-2$ and $g(3)=1.$ Since $-1\\in [-2,1],$  the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[3,4],$ we have $g(3)=1$ and $g(4)=-3.$ Since $-1\\in [-3,1],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[4,5],$ we have $g(4)=-3$ and $g(5)=1.$ Since $-1\\in [-3,1],$ the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[5,6],$ we have $g(5)=1$ and $g(6)=0.$ Since $-1\\notin [0,1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[6,7],$ we have $g(6)=0$ and $g(7)=2.$ Since $-1\\notin [0,2],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=-1$ on the subintervals $[2,3],$ $[3,4]$  and $[4,5].$ Therefore, $g(x)=-1$ has at least three solutions on $[2,7].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd0e4cbd-0cf5-5247-bb9f-42420165d644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344993} {:answer/id #uuid "502822aa-bdd4-5e02-8621-c427876da4bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-1,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344994} {:answer/id #uuid "5a65cea0-8ee6-5597-b009-0b07d92e7328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-4,-2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344995} {:answer/id #uuid "94c8694a-e26a-5ff3-8392-10ee69cb0113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-5,-3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344996} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe959cd-e4ab-592a-8976-7e1d2bdcb7dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-8,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "502822aa-bdd4-5e02-8621-c427876da4bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-1,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344994} :answer-field/id #uuid "f316c664-e162-5146-9216-285295eb0098" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc3e6bf9-50aa-5413-821b-b19c043d5f9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-105710" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+5x+6}?$" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which makes the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have ${x}^{2}+5x+6=0,$ which corresponds to $x=-3$ or $x=-2.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the points $x=-3$ or $x=-2.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-1,3]$ is allowed. The intervals $[-8,-3)$ and $(-2,1]$ are not closed, while $[-5,-3]$ and $[-4,-2]$ contain the point $x=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Number of Solutions Given a Table of Values of a Continuous Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "248186dc-69bc-588d-af4f-ea72c61b7aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "248186dc-69bc-588d-af4f-ea72c61b7aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345000} :answer-field/id #uuid "3de3cae7-60c1-589f-b727-08a1c3ebacba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efc73626-18fa-5f9c-868c-bc8028827690" :question/math-academy-id "q-344761" :question/problem "The function $p(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-6,5].$ Some values of $p(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-6$ | $-2$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p(x)$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $p(x)=2?$\n\nThe equation $p(x)=2$ has at least {{field-1}} solutions." :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a continuous function on $[-6,5],$ we can apply the Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT) in each subinterval of $[-6,5].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-6,5]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $p(x)=2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-6,-2],$ we have $p(-6)=4$ and $p(-2)=-1.$ Since $2\\in [-1,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $p(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-2,5],$ we have $p(-2)=-1$ and $p(5)=-3.$ Since $2\\notin [-3,-1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $p(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $p(x)=2$ on $[-6,-2].$  \n\nTherefore, $p(x)=2$ has at least $1$ solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63a4ae8c-4442-5de5-94b6-232ef617a28f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345003} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ad231f-aed0-54ef-ae98-6e126fd6c504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345004} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ae97dc-9ad5-5174-b76e-8850f13d23f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345005} {:answer/id #uuid "8610a7aa-bf37-5c1d-bb95-fbcd49ab1177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345006} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d80a1c-a681-5a79-9f63-0925c626bd3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4ad231f-aed0-54ef-ae98-6e126fd6c504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345004} :answer-field/id #uuid "44ed66c8-4595-521b-a539-85b6b3486dcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "607354a6-04e1-596c-a6fa-ef51f5a0c65a" :question/math-academy-id "q-47016" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to this function?\n\n- $[0,1]$\n- $[1,2]$\n- $[-1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied on closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nThe function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $, where $n$ is any integer.\n\n- On the interval $[0,1],$ we **can** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- On the interval $[1,2],$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval contains the point of discontinuity $\\frac{\\pi }{2}≈1.57\\,.$\n\n- On the interval $[-1,0),$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval is not closed.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c89ecba-2b3a-5af9-b420-83b9f1c9ce27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345010} {:answer/id #uuid "45482e8d-300f-5066-899c-7ada6a2e9c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345011} {:answer/id #uuid "8c71113e-6167-5212-9e84-d7f3e2a1b171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345012} {:answer/id #uuid "5da618d3-2d1b-5826-b019-3524d0f29c75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345013} {:answer/id #uuid "c87c726b-1a25-56f4-9a96-2cca1824ab4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45482e8d-300f-5066-899c-7ada6a2e9c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345011} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c476131-9a6d-5be8-bed4-8c96f813dc16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2fb92b7b-dc10-5228-9860-a0045fd149f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-47035" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x(x+3)(x-2).$ On which of the following intervals does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that the equation $f(x)=-5$ has a solution?\n\n- $[-5,-3]$\n- $[-3,-2]$\n- $[-2,0]$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is a polynomial, it's continuous over the real numbers. Thus, we can apply the intermediate value theorem to any of the given intervals.\n\nLet's make a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the endpoints.\n\n| $x$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-70$ | $0$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\n- On the interval $[-5,-3],$ we have $f(-5)=-70$ and $f(-3)=0.$ Since $-5\\in [-70,0],$ the IVT does guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[-3,-2],$ we have $f(-3)=0$ and $f(-2)=8.$ Since $-5\\notin [0,8],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[-2,0],$ we have $f(-2)=8$ and $f(0)=0.$ Since $-5\\notin [0,8],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1adffa92-2a25-54f0-a38d-b3db45a7d65c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345017} {:answer/id #uuid "6f411143-f108-53f2-85c4-09d79aa535f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345018} {:answer/id #uuid "ad8eb979-e97f-5324-9249-e882c3f93a3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345019} {:answer/id #uuid "cd90d2dc-2192-55f8-9f49-2009b262e204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345020} {:answer/id #uuid "64ac0818-2e3b-543b-a8cd-d46bd1c275c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64ac0818-2e3b-543b-a8cd-d46bd1c275c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345021} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b333f02-6466-58f8-9986-a863aba1c44b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "697ba05b-3399-5081-bfb5-548a407d6a14" :question/math-academy-id "q-47055" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}.$ In which of the following intervals does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that the equation $f(x)=3$ has a solution? \n\n- $[-4,-1]$\n- $[3,5]$\n- $[-1,4]$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=2,$ and so the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=2$ \n\nLet's make a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the endpoints.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0$ | $4$ | $2.5$ | $2$ |\n\n- On the interval $[-4,-1],$ we have $f(-4)=0.5$ and $f(-1)=0.$ Since $3\\notin [0,0.5],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[3,5],$ we have $f(3)=4$ and $f(5)=2.$ Since $3\\in [2,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,4]$ contains the point $x=2.$ Therefore we can not apply the intermediate value theorem on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II  only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Number of Solutions Given a Table of Values of a Continuous Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcac1b18-cba9-569a-bc93-42a1315b185e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345024} {:answer/id #uuid "597bab38-2d9b-588c-a7f5-61c88180e414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345025} {:answer/id #uuid "46a00ddd-ce6d-521f-92c2-7d27cd7c051c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345026} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e4ca36-287a-559d-8dad-2573df10897d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345027} {:answer/id #uuid "2178564b-e988-5ca6-bac5-e94928b8d1bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcac1b18-cba9-569a-bc93-42a1315b185e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345024} :answer-field/id #uuid "661a7cd6-21e2-5ad0-ae21-0d83b89886d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8186883d-213b-5f00-9b7b-9a8de4d9c22e" :question/math-academy-id "q-47056" :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-4,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-5$ | $2$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-4,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-4,2].$\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[-4,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=1.$\n\n- On the subinterval $[-4,-3],$ we have $g(-4)=2$ and $g(-3)=-1.$ Since $1\\in [-1,2],$  the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-3,-1],$ we have $g(-3)=-1$ and $g(-1)=-5.$ Since $1\\notin [-5,-1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,0],$ we have $g(-1)=-5$ and $g(0)=2.$ Since $1\\in [-5,2],$ the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[0,1],$ we have $g(0)=2$ and $g(1)=3.$ Since $1\\notin [2,3],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[1,2],$ we have $g(1)=3$ and $g(2)=0.$ Since $1\\in [0,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=1$ on the subintervals $[-4,-3],$ $[-1,0],$  and $[1,2].$ Therefore, $g(x)=1$ has at least three solutions on $[-4,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Selecting a Specific Value For Which a Solution Is Guaranteed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82bf6727-4d46-53cc-bd94-e1fb9aeb88de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132813} {:answer/id #uuid "88b6380f-e14b-55cd-8abc-53e091d1b0bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k =-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132814} {:answer/id #uuid "b125e1ed-834c-589a-98d8-1461d2f86062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132815} {:answer/id #uuid "ec348464-4f61-5199-a95b-bcd9353d2446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132816} {:answer/id #uuid "65ed2b30-a74a-573b-911b-be1e328a7d17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k =-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b125e1ed-834c-589a-98d8-1461d2f86062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132815} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c4077f6-7b12-53f5-bb48-f706b93e197a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f019924-a46f-5bc3-be22-35c5680cdf03" :question/math-academy-id "q-47058" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=(x+1)^{2}-4.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $x\\in [-4,-2]?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-4,-2],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-4)$ and $f(-2).$\n\nSince $f(-4)=5$ and $f(-2)=-3,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-3,5].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=2$ belongs to the interval $[-3,5].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "265faa65-e740-5f6a-adaf-a25daec9852f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-2,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345031} {:answer/id #uuid "344c5f03-f255-5fdd-9978-57b07397eecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-2,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345032} {:answer/id #uuid "7da63e35-551e-597e-8680-25e779f087cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345033} {:answer/id #uuid "8eaa55f4-8be3-5d67-b223-84127e46ec87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345034} {:answer/id #uuid "06bc2954-d765-5a6a-919f-8b4d0f08e6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "265faa65-e740-5f6a-adaf-a25daec9852f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-2,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345031} :answer-field/id #uuid "6057ac87-2a36-51ab-a6bc-c8d90498e45f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f66078a-9fc7-5c7e-ae29-7151d1232eff" :question/math-academy-id "q-47077" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 5-2x, & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function?" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at $x=1.$ To see this, we note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=2\\ne 3={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=1.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-2,0]$ is allowed.  The intervals $(1,3]$ and $[-2,1)$ are not closed, $[-2,1]$ and $[0,3]$ contain the point $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Selecting a Specific Value For Which a Solution Is Guaranteed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7cbd672-6dbd-5554-a5e7-727e568c1715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345038} {:answer/id #uuid "661c0894-ca97-55ba-9aac-3361522c8a66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345039} {:answer/id #uuid "1d0bf84e-e96e-5a48-bce4-0c9cbdae7676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345040} {:answer/id #uuid "48d33e0a-d0d6-5858-b4eb-8d2c71c47076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345041} {:answer/id #uuid "a7267ca4-4925-59fc-8266-e85d1d9604de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "661c0894-ca97-55ba-9aac-3361522c8a66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345039} :answer-field/id #uuid "62991643-477b-5614-a062-47ec1b3dd459" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f05bf34-eff5-5d2f-b84c-b025710dd1a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-47101" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x)=(x-2)(x+2)(x-5).$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem **not** guarantee that $g(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $[2,4]?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function $g(x)$ is continuous on $[2,4]$, the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $g(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $g(2)$ and $g(4).$\n\nSince $g(2)=0$ and $g(4)=-12,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-12,0].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=1$ does not belong to the interval $[-12,0].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d01d143-68f2-534a-8be6-4f63f9ab0d40" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement On the Intermediate Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2da35f88-333e-5d07-b9de-9604a067fa9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345045} {:answer/id #uuid "11b1723c-5d86-51c5-8633-518ec23f09b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345046} {:answer/id #uuid "eee04c3c-1c3a-595a-aa01-071c368aeaec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345047} {:answer/id #uuid "92572270-e0a4-5e1e-b484-e1d56730900c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345048} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0e2eba-fe42-571c-a15e-08c6a71c5df3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eee04c3c-1c3a-595a-aa01-071c368aeaec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345047} :answer-field/id #uuid "043588dc-488f-55b9-97f9-145d2b5125d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dedd176b-2816-5c53-a9f1-afe51d898c52" :question/math-academy-id "q-48334" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/2dd5fb6cdd180dffc3f4ad5c1742b0fc60ecaf31e5ad6c299c101c44a7f6654b.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[-2,3].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [-2,3]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,3],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-2)$ and $f(3)$ inclusively.\n\nSince $f(-2)=6$ and $f(3)=2$, we must have \n$k\\in [2,6].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$2$ and $6$\".\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/567a6a601c80d1af5adcb10188d6ae4de83aa154505d59945cadbc226f05eb4b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d01d143-68f2-534a-8be6-4f63f9ab0d40" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement On the Intermediate Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e8cd68e-a8ef-5127-95ed-9b0842bcb7f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c\\in (-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345052} {:answer/id #uuid "94ff9198-9947-5944-97a9-0dd9dfcf8170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c\\in [-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345053} {:answer/id #uuid "6470bc4a-3c6f-5070-a2b5-42c2f3a02b76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c\\in [1,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345054} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc6a1ed-4a6e-58e5-96da-4135e6742d44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c\\in (1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345055} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8f2b32-52bb-5576-8aad-782e67498f4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c\\in [-2,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb8f2b32-52bb-5576-8aad-782e67498f4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c\\in [-2,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345056} :answer-field/id #uuid "87093c70-1501-5edf-80d2-2485e63c4a02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23995f2c-e036-515a-946d-e63146f2696f" :question/math-academy-id "q-48361" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/4906a1de7cc1eb78bbff845fe6b03b024b47c46e84c801609e8da40a5d37c864.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[-2,2].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $1$ and $2$ inclusively, then there exists a number $\\text{ ________ }$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" :question/worked-solution "The $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$, the intermediate value theorem states that \n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $f(-2)$ and $f(2)$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [-2,2]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nFrom the graph we see that $f(-2)=1$ and $f(2)=2.$Therefore, the missing part is \"$c\\in [-2,2]$\".\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/455863da8f126505f018a356f5f89bbbea81f1e1e90e803a51390bed22514a9a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d01d143-68f2-534a-8be6-4f63f9ab0d40" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement On the Intermediate Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b9fd23-d80c-56fe-800b-92c6175dea1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345059} {:answer/id #uuid "12d7e2c3-7707-595e-a381-e64804f9a760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345060} {:answer/id #uuid "a6dae852-b0c9-5aa8-b216-bf489c47f1b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345061} {:answer/id #uuid "26149fe6-1006-5add-8412-3f550386c1c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345062} {:answer/id #uuid "b3bd694c-f78c-5678-8869-a2d5dc564a09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26149fe6-1006-5add-8412-3f550386c1c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345062} :answer-field/id #uuid "4890ae8e-40df-5695-900c-0620b15c647b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "13761308-9fdd-5204-a133-f89f8f864d21" :question/math-academy-id "q-48429" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}+x$ which is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[1,8].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [1,8]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[1,8],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(1)$ and $f(8).$\n\nSince $f(1)=2$ and $f(8)=10$, we must have \n$k\\in [2,10].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$2$ and $10$\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d01d143-68f2-534a-8be6-4f63f9ab0d40" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement On the Intermediate Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3e7a00a-d4ec-585a-9d57-81f945bad7a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345066} {:answer/id #uuid "2456ac11-e89b-51d6-8093-6004f02adc8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345067} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f19b5f-bb77-5c93-8d82-95b55f55caf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345068} {:answer/id #uuid "6d43b8c1-c699-5951-80f5-98d285b65b76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III, II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345069} {:answer/id #uuid "fdba5118-c578-5d61-a859-667b43543448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdba5118-c578-5d61-a859-667b43543448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345070} :answer-field/id #uuid "a02ee324-a714-50cd-9690-cb1111b82ecb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78f4f066-a6ea-5590-a2c5-0ffdcc3f7ecb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48430" :question/problem "A statement of the intermediate value theorem is given below but some of its parts are missing.\n\n*Let $f(x)$ be a function that is $\\text{ ______ }$ on a closed interval $[a,b].$ If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ _________ }$, then there exists a number $c\\in [a,b]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nGoing from left to right, in which order should the parts I-III, listed below, be placed into the blank spaces to complete the statement of the theorem?\n\n- continuous\n- $f(a)$ and $f(b)$ inclusively\n- defined" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem states the following.\n\n*Let $f(x)$ be a function that is **continuous** on a closed interval $[a,b].$ If $k$ is a number that lies between $f(a)$ **and** $f(b)$ **inclusively**, then there exists a number $c\\in [a,b]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Selecting a Specific Value For Which a Solution Is Guaranteed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e854a031-024a-5623-8749-d16f08921493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345073} {:answer/id #uuid "2127dff1-25ba-5de5-a883-1cf12d569b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345074} {:answer/id #uuid "fbae1afc-7521-5345-811c-6a890bc02c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345075} {:answer/id #uuid "e6de78da-bb34-5669-b5e8-0071290e17a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345076} {:answer/id #uuid "e1406abd-fde3-5dd7-80b2-2353e6ae804c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2127dff1-25ba-5de5-a883-1cf12d569b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345074} :answer-field/id #uuid "858dae43-c314-54fe-93dd-7bad6af7bf05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fb52c76-7e7e-5fff-a905-fce89cd57965" :question/math-academy-id "q-78510" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=8-(x+2)^{2}.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $x\\in [-2,1]?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,1],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-2)$ and $f(1).$\n\nSince $f(-2)=8$ and $f(1)=-1,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-1,8].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=5$ belongs to the interval $[-1,8].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/4b9b0cecb305a49f98b7b7a6a2ebca69c1ce10acdb72cb88d805e0f1901dc882.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Number of Solutions Given a Table of Values of a Continuous Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d451ce96-5287-58be-b94e-8e041ca0b047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345080} {:answer/id #uuid "c786ebaa-6864-5bdf-a4b3-f0bbf9c7aeb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345081} {:answer/id #uuid "2cff2729-d494-5f57-8918-7b167f12f627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345082} {:answer/id #uuid "dd262be0-5e1e-524e-9218-f4bcc266ba64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345083} {:answer/id #uuid "e23cfe1d-ebd5-592f-b1a0-1a58e6bda67a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d451ce96-5287-58be-b94e-8e041ca0b047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345080} :answer-field/id #uuid "f99c1c86-001c-57f6-b815-e2f99a7fd7ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c832a0ab-c8ed-58dd-a542-b7df2a173528" :question/math-academy-id "q-78537" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [2,7].$ Some values of $f(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $f(x)=2?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is a continuous function on $[2,7]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[2,7].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[2,7]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $f(x)=2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[2,3],$ we have $f(2)=3$ and $f(3)=1.$ Since $2\\in [1,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[3,6],$ we have $f(3)=1$ and $f(6)=4.$ Since $2\\in [1,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[6,7],$ we have $f(6)=4$ and $f(7)=0.$ Since $2\\in [0,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $f(x)=2$ on the subintervals $[2,3],$ $[3,6]$ and $[6,7].$  Therefore, $f(x)=2$ has at least three solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Number of Solutions Given a Table of Values of a Continuous Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f4441f5-dc2d-5e71-ab3e-a57b7a0fe01d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345087} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf50740-3fea-51db-bc11-dbc18ddd17cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345088} {:answer/id #uuid "0c379d93-f7e2-5e39-837e-57b2d4f9010b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345089} {:answer/id #uuid "48cb2c3b-dffd-557f-bd70-629eb3b2c000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345090} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9ee9ef-0754-5612-bf7b-3fc3ee107458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cf50740-3fea-51db-bc11-dbc18ddd17cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345088} :answer-field/id #uuid "173e3de0-c9dd-5b6a-aeb4-f1f28a1cd4ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2993211-5bf5-5455-954d-b0eb6a795598" :question/math-academy-id "q-78542" :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-1,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $2$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-1,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-1,2].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-1,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,1],$ we have $g(-1)=2$ and $g(1)=0.$ Since $1\\in [0,2],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[1,2],$ we have $g(1)=0$ and $g(2)=3.$ Since $1\\in [0,3],$ the IVT  *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=1$ on the subintervals $[-1,1]$ and $[1,2].$ Therefore, $g(x)=1$ has at least two solutions."}]}
